---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
import LF from "../../components/LF.astro"
import S from "../../components/S.astro"
import Clock from "../../components/Clock.jsx"
---
<Slide t1="Technology Introduction" splash={ true } t2="Software and Systems">
    <ul>
        <LF>Open source vs. commercial</LF>
        <LF>Web vs. native desktop vs. mobile app (vs. terminal/command-line)</LF>
        <LF>Standalone vs. networked (where is the data?)</LF>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Web Architectures">
    <ul>
        <LF>Linked hypertext (hence, "The Web")</LF>
        <LF>Content distributed across multiple linked servers (decentralised&mdash;in theory)</LF>
        <LF>Server as content store and service provider</LF>
        <LF>Browser as endpoint:
            <ul>
                <LF>Able to download, format and display content</LF>
                <LF>Able to run code (sandboxed - untrusted)</LF>
                <LF>Thin client (&#x1F632;)</LF>
                <LF>No (or hardly any) data storage</LF>
            </ul>
        </LF>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t1="Web Technologies 101" splash={ true } t2="The Languages of the Web">
    <dl class="text-sm-2">
        <dt class="fragment">HTML</dt>
        <dd class="fragment">Hypertext markup language: textual content, structural elements, links</dd>

        <dt class="fragment">CSS</dt>
        <dd class="fragment">Cascading style sheets: formatting, size, colour, layout</dd>

        <dt class="fragment">SVG</dt>
        <dd class="fragment">Scalable Vector Graphics (more niche): geometric graphics markup</dd>

        <dt class="fragment">JavaScript</dt>
        <dd class="fragment">The scripting language of the web browser</dd>

        <dt class="fragment"><span class="text-grey">WebAssembly</span></dt>
        <dd class="fragment text-grey">The assembly language of the web browser (nascent)</dd>
    </dl>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="The Back-End Languages of the Web">
    <dl class="text-sm-1">
        <div class="fragment">
            <dt>Java, Python, Ruby, PHP (&#129326;), C/C++, JavaScript, ...</dt>
            <dd>The menagerie of languages used to build web servers and services</dd>
        </div>

        <div class="fragment">
            <dt>SQL, GraphQL, NoSQL, Pandas, ...</dt>
            <dd>Data organisation and query languages</dd>
        </div>
    </dl>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Where Things Run">
    <dl class="text-sm-1">
        <div class="fragment">
            <dt>Server-Side</dt>
            <dd>Authentication/authorisation, networked APIs, databases, page rendering,
                media support, ...</dd>
        </div>

        <div class="fragment">
            <dt>Client-Side</dt>
            <dd>Dynamic pages: interaction, adaptation, rich media,
                page rendering, local evaluation and refresh (<Clock client:visible />)
            </dd>
        </div>
    </dl>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Who Owns / Controls What?">
    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <LF><S t="orange">Server</S> (data, workflow, access, ...): service provider</LF>
        <LF><S t="orange">Browser</S> (presentation, local interaction): service user</LF>
        <LF><em>But</em>: vendors can require JavaScript to control browser use
            (ref: <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2025/01/18/google-search-javascript">Daring Fireball</a>
            re: Google)</LF>
    </ul>
</Slide>
